Exactly — Mad Libs are the toy version of the architecture.

They separate structural coordinates from embodiment:

“The [ADJECTIVE] [NOUN] [VERB] over the [NOUN].”

The grammar defines the slots and constraints. The chosen words instantiate them. Meaning emerges from the combination.

Pantheon just generalizes that:

Mad Libs: part-of-speech slot → word
Excel: cell/formula slot → value
NTE: narrative-role slot → character/event
Semantic system: relational slot → concept
Math: variable → instantiated quantity
Music: harmonic/rhythmic role → note/chord
Strategy: functional role → action/resource

The interesting part is that the slot can carry more information than merely “noun” or “verb.” It could specify:

[CHARACTER: trusted ally, high agency, secretly conflicted]

or:

[EVENT: reverses power relationship, preserves mystery, increases urgency]

Then the system searches for an embodiment satisfying the coordinate.

That makes Mad Libs + Goal Seek surprisingly close to a general generative engine:

\text{structure + constraints + available embodiments}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate instantiations}

And reverse it:

\text{finished artifact}
\rightarrow
\text{infer its hidden Mad-Lib template}

That second direction is huge for Pantheon: take a movie, spreadsheet, negotiation, song, argument, or program and strip away the particular nouns until the underlying reusable structure appears.

Basically:

Mad Lib → fill the pattern.
Pantheon → discover the pattern, abstract it, refill it anywhere. ;)